Report: Integer From conversions and generic bitwise result preservation
66 claims; 6 removal(s).
  run_ids: ["00506d31-6b43-43b9-a10e-af38e3e0e56f"] -> ["45fba944-37c1-4602-9434-11e4f137651d"]
  title: "Bitwise wrapper operations preserve the underlying operator result" -> "Integer From conversions and generic bitwise result preservation"
  explanation: "For endian-type 0.2.0, the handwritten BitAnd, BitOr, and BitXor implementations of BigEndian<T> and LittleEndian<T> apply the corresponding underlying T operator and wrap its successful output. Each of the six proved contracts quantifies arbitrary operand and output types, operands, and operator dictionaries: the translated wrapper result equals the underlying operator result. This includes propagation of failure or divergence represented in the Aeneas result model. The wrappers add no independent failure in that model. These contracts prove the unchanged published wrapper bodies; they do not prove any user-supplied T operator implementation." -> "For endian-type 0.2.0, six generic BigEndian/ LittleEndian bitwise methods preserve the underlying operator result. Sixty integer From method bodies additionally implement exact identity or byte reversal under explicitly supplied x86_64 little-endian scalar-operation models. LittleEndian construction and conversion to an integer preserve the stored word; BigEndian construction and conversion to an integer reverse its bytes; cross-wrapper conversion reverses the stored word. These contracts cover every bit pattern of u16/u32/u64/u128/usize and i16/i32/i64/i128/isize. Pointer-sized Lean theorems explicitly require System.Platform.numBits=64. Original source and Cargo.toml are unchanged. Fifty executable external-operation models compose official byte primitives and are proved against independent bitvector specifications; Kani separately checks native Rust operations against explicit reversed-byte references for all inputs. Separate ordinary Kani harnesses directly check forty concrete integer byte APIs (ten harnesses, 220 checks) and sixty integer instantiations of six slice methods (1030 checks, including 180 specification assertions and 20 unsupported-path guards). The slice checks are limited to initialized, correctly aligned exact-length repr(C) leading-field buffers with arbitrary contents and replacement values; they do not establish arbitrary-T, arbitrary allocation-context, invalid/short/misaligned-buffer, or Rust aliasing guarantees. These supplemental Kani checks are not among the 66 Aeneas machine claims."
  trusted_assumptions: "The Rust compiler, Charon/Aeneas translation, their standard-library models, and the Lean kernel are trusted. Underlying operator behavior is represented by an arbitrary trait dictionary, rather than asserted to implement a particular integer operation. The theorems have only propext, Classical.choice, and Quot.sound as transitive axioms." -> "The Rust compiler, Charon/Aeneas, official scalar/byte primitives, Lean kernel, Kani and CBMC are trusted. Fifty native-endian std-operation models are explicitly supplied. Their Rust byte formulas and official Lean byte primitives are connected by review, not a mechanically checked cross-language theorem; std operator bodies are not independently proved in Lean. Kani and extraction use different Rust nightlies. The six bitwise results quantify arbitrary underlying operator dictionaries without proving user callback bodies. All 66 API contracts and 50 model specifications use only propext, Classical.choice and Quot.sound as transitive axioms."
  environment: "Unmodified crates.io endian-type 0.2.0, no_std library, x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu. Rust nightly-2026-09-17; Charon 08d7c9d50144bd84848212c24701253585e90d1f; Aeneas be86ab0f1989d04944292b345b24c435be3ad10a; Lean 4.35.0. Official method-root selection, no source/IR/tool modifications. Six generic method-body equality theorems. cargo-proofs API discovery uses a documented official-rustc macro-expansion compatibility fallback and recognizes cfg(doctest) as false; proof compilation, exactly-one API resolution, and axiom policy are unchanged. Exact standard bit-operator catalogue basename aliases preserve distinct self type/method/ID matching." -> "Original crates.io endian-type 0.2.0; x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu little-endian. Rust nightly-2026-09-17; Charon 08d7c9d50144bd84848212c24701253585e90d1f; Aeneas/official library be86ab0f1989d04944292b345b24c435be3ad10a; Lean 4.35.0. Official method roots verify the six generic methods. A pure caller driver with --include endian_type extracts all sixty actual dependency bodies. Official compiler source-path remapping supports reproducible spans. Exact model/function/type/use guards and transitive axiom audits are retained. Kani 0.68.0/CBMC 6.11.0/Rust nightly-2026-08-21: ten symbolic std-model harnesses, 216 checks, zero failures."
  evidence_url: "https://github.com/nyuichi/rust-crate-proofs/tree/3213c669b1a3c5d8448b2750849526c388250f54" -> "https://github.com/nyuichi/rust-crate-proofs/tree/e030b7f56f12a473c15376f01bb53e7920892b20"
  limitations: "Only six handwritten generic bitwise operator method bodies are covered. No endian conversion, integer From implementation, byte representation, raw-pointer/slice access, transmute safety, derived standard trait implementation, or complete crate memory-safety claim is established. Concrete integer operator dictionaries are not independently proved here. Extraction/build used x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu; other targets were not checked." -> "Coverage is limited to six bitwise methods and sixty concrete integer From implementations on the stated target/model domain. The forty-six byte/slice APIs and their unsafe layout/validity/aliasing obligations are not covered by these Aeneas claims. No generic arbitrary-T memory-safety, other target, endian, or pointer-width claim is established. Derived standard-trait methods are excluded. Remaining trait/default/blanket behavior is not verified. Type-specialized Rust identities and any erased server catalogue keys must be distinguished."
